    "lawCode": "Amd §66-j, Pub Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Public Service Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6919\n\nSPONSOR: KENNEDY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public service law, in relation to allowing certain\ncustomer-generators who generate solar electricity to donate credit for\nsuch electricity to low-income families\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill allows certain customer-generators who generate solar elec-\ntricity to donate credit for such electricity to low-income families.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends paragraph (b) of subdivision 4 of section 66-j of the\npublic service law, as amended by chapter 494 of the laws of 2014, and a\nnew paragraph (e) is added. Section two provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrently, residential solar panels are net-metered and excess energy\nproduced by a rooftop solar system is converted to 'credits'. Those\ncredits can be used toward future electric bills by the owner but cannot\nbe cashed out nor can the credits be transferred to others. Some rooftop\nsolar owners produce more electricity than they consume annually, mean-\ning they effectively donate that energy to the power companies. A system\nthat produces -3000 kWh of net energy in excess of what is used over a\nyear is enough energy to power a small family home for 4-5 months.\nCustomer-generators should have the option to donate this excess to a\npool of credits to help families in need. NYSERDA currently estimates\nthat 86,000 homes in the state of New York are powered by residential\nsolar systems. If each of those homes donated just 1000 kWh of excess\nenergy credits it would be enough to power approximately 12,500 homes\nfor an entire year. This bill will keep the lights on by helping reduce\nenergy costs for families in need; fight climate change by helping meet\nthe state's ambitious clean energy goals and further incentivize clean\nenergy for New York residents; create jobs by increasing the potential\nsize of residential solar projects and have no cost to New York State.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately\n",
