 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Allows certain customer-generators who generate solar electricity to donate credit for such electricity to low-income families; requires electric corporations to create a low-income energy assistance pool for the donation of such credits.",
 "title": "Allows certain customer-generators who generate solar electricity to donate credit for such electricity to low-income families",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "S6919",
     "basePrintNoStr": "S6919-2021",
     "printNo": "S6919",
     "session": 2021,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": " \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 182\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nSenate Bill Number 6919, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend  the public service law, in relation to allowing\n      certain customer-generators  who  generate  solar  electricity  to\n      donate credit for such electricity to low-income families\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This  bill would authorize customer-generators to donate excess energy\ncredits to a low-income energy assistance pool to be established by  the\nPublic Service Commission (PSC). Further, it would require those credits\nbe  valued  at  the  same  rate  applicable to service provided to other\ncustomers in the same service class that do not generate electricity.\n\n  The Department of Public Service and the PSC work  to  balance  public\ninterests  in  rate cases, including cost allocations, rate designs, and\nspecial programs to protect low-income customers.  In  addition  to  the\nState's overall efforts to transition the electric grid to 70% renewable\nenergy  by  2030  and  100%  zero  emissions  by 2040, there are already\nseveral programs designed to specifically bring the benefits of  renewa-\nble energy to low-income consumers. Additionally, there are bill payment\nassistance  programs  that  lower  the  costs of their energy, including\nNY-Sun Solar Energy Equity Framework (SEEF), NY-Sun SEEF  for  Community\nSolar,  Expanded  Solar  for  All, and the Customer Benefit Contribution\n(CBC). The CBC is a new program developed through  an  extensive  stake-\nholder engagement process to address equity issues in the implementation\nof New York's ambitious solar goals. The CBC seeks to address this ineq-\nuity  by  increasing  the  amount that customers with on-site generation\ncontribute to these programs.\n \n  These existing programs are proven, effective  mechanisms  to  provide\naffordable  clean  energy  to  New Yorkers. The initiative that would be\ncreated under this bill would unfortunately struggle with  efficacy,  as\nthere  are  not many unused energy credits to begin with, and any energy\ncredits that go unused reduce the cost that would have been  imposed  on\nother residential customers. The State's existing programs can be lever-\naged  to provide assistance to low-income families. This bill would also\ncreate an unnecessary accounting and billing function  that  would  cost\nthe  PSC  money, and would not result in any meaningful benefits to low-\nincome consumers.\n \n  Therefore, I am constrained to veto this bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                       (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "KATHY HOCHUL",
